One-Step Triage Commands (Fast Sorting)

Option 1: Instantly Find All Hidden Files (Most Efficient)
Instead of looking for empty folders visually, tell the system to find all hidden items across all subdirectories
simultaneously:
      find . -type f -name ".*"
How it works: 
It scans from your current location (.), filters only for files (-type f), and matches names starting with a dot
(-name ".*"). This will immediately output the exact path, such as ./Documents/to-do/.hidden_file.zip, 
ignoring all the non-hidden noise.

Option 2: Filtered Tree View
If you still want the visual context of where the folder sits in the directory structure, make tree include 
hidden files but use grep to filter out everything else:
      tree -a | grep -E "\/\."
How it works: 
The -a flag forces tree to see hidden items. The grep pipe filters the output to only show paths containing 
hidden folders or files (indicated by a dot). You will instantly see the hidden archive inside the to-do folder.

Option 3: Find Hidden Files + Identify Real File Type (Pro Move)
The challenge description mentions incorrect file extensions. Attackers often hide a zip file by renaming it to
something like .system_log.txt.This powerful one-liner finds all hidden files and immediately reveals their true
magic bytes (file signature):
      find . -type f -name ".*" -exec file {} +
How it works: 
The output will look like ./to-do/.evidence.txt: Zip archive data.... Even if the extension is faked, the file 
command uncovers that it is actually a ZIP archive 
